diagnosis_text:
The ticket alleges that PC4 (Location A, 172.16.10.4/24, VLAN 10) has lost connectivity to Laptop0 (172.16.20.10/24, VLAN 20), and that "the box that joins the two halves" — referring to the inter-VLAN routing device — has died. Direct measurement on the live lab shows this claim has no basis.

PC4 and Laptop0 are in two different IP subnets (172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24), both reachable via switch1 → mswitch1 → router0. Router0 implements a classic router-on-a-stick design: its eth0 physical port is sub-divided into VLAN subinterfaces eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) and eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24), which serve as the default gateways for the two VLANs respectively. mswitch1 bridges the VLAN-tagged trunk between switch1 and router0 using br_vlan10 and br_vlan20 bridges, all of which are in an UP state.

Active ping tests confirm the entire inter-VLAN path is fully functional: PC4 reaches Laptop0 with 0% packet loss and TTL=63 (correct one-hop decrement through router0), both end-hosts reach their respective gateways with TTL=64 (direct, on-device response), and a second pair (pc5 → laptop2) also passes without loss. There are no dropped packets, no interface faults, and no routing anomalies.

Root cause: There is no fault. The network is operating as designed; the user's report is a false premise. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===